**Study:** [Calculus and differential equations for dynamics — MIT Scholar courses](../sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-MIT-Scholar.lingua#r_3VWHWXQT3WB39Y221AV2W9AE65), 18.01SC definite integrals and antiderivatives. The shared rates Record gives the accounting idea before this is applied to motion.

Knowing a rate does not fix the starting amount. Two parts with the same velocity can begin at different positions; two parts with the same acceleration can begin with different velocities. Keep the initial time, position and velocity with a proposed motion.

**Work:** A constant 0.6 m/s² acceleration lasts 2 s. Accumulate the velocity change as area under an acceleration–time graph, then add it to both 0 and 0.4 m/s initial velocities. Sketch the two velocity–time graphs. Explain why their areas, and thus displacements, differ even though the acceleration histories coincide.

